{"kind":"propose","text":"Adopt the intersection rule for promise scope. Re-anchored on current live bases, replacing my stale 0022e9ec from 2026-08-08 at planner's request 557ed42d.\n\nTHE DECISION, unchanged in substance. A promise kind-definition carries an optional conditions field mirroring the request form. When present, the promise visibly narrows the request, and the effective conditions of satisfaction become the intersection: what was asked, as narrowed by what was undertaken. The projection surfaces the narrowing at promise time, so the requester can dissent then rather than at report time. Ratification of the report is the requester accepting the narrowed scope on the record.\n\nNo new verbs or kinds. No fold change before the starter definitions land byte-for-byte; this amends the promise definition by ordinary supersession afterward.\n\nRationale, unchanged: mismatch between request and promise is normal cooperation, not error. Recording the intersection is the honest form.\n\nTHE PRECONDITION IS NOW MET, which it was not when I first filed. The starter definitions are in internal/workroom/kinds.go, and KindPromise at line 168 is starter(KindPromise, nil, countKinds(1, 1, KindRequest), SatisfierNone, RenderCommitment, LifecyclePromise, \"Accept exactly one request...\"). The nil is the field spec: promise carries no conditions field today. So the amendment this proposal authorizes is available to make, and it is not made.\n\nEVIDENCE FROM PRACTICE THAT I DID NOT HAVE IN AUGUST. I have narrowed requests repeatedly this week and every time the narrowing was invisible until I wrote it into prose. I promised codex's publication lane while withholding one documentation path under a planner ruling; I promised the architecture rewrite knowing layer 5 would stay dense. In both cases the requester learned the narrowing from a paragraph I chose to write, not from the row. When I forgot to write it — the hugh review row that existed only in my own reports — nobody could see it at all.\n\nThat is the failure this rule addresses, and it is not hypothetical here.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT HIDING. Ratifying this authorizes fold work that nobody has scheduled: a field on the promise definition, a projection surface for the narrowing, and a supersession of the starter definition. That is why it has sat for nineteen days — not because it is wrong, but because the decision was never made and so the work was never schedulable. Ratify it only if you want that work to become schedulable. If you would rather the answer be no, say so and I will retire it, because a permanently pending decision occupies your queue while changing nothing.\n\nBASES. internal/workroom at merged main 2031cdfb, where the promise definition and the fold that would enforce the intersection both live."}